{"id": 20, "ts": 1779497424.5768352, "event": "public_discussion_message", "round": 1, "agent": "agent_2", "message": "Based on the evidence we have, Candidate\u202fC still appears to be the strongest overall fit: 100\u202f% reliability, a consistently positive crew atmosphere, quick and correct decision\u2011making, and calmness under pressure. The primary remaining concern is his difficulty communicating ideas and the perception of egocentricity, which could affect cockpit coordination on long\u2011distance flights.\nCandidate\u202fA brings excellent technical competence (anticipates danger, sees complex connections, strong spatial vision, leadership) but is viewed as a show\u2011off and not open to new ideas, and we still lack any observations on his crew\u2011cooperation or professional communication.\nCandidate\u202fB is highly conscientious and handles stress well, yet his \u201cnasty remarks\u201d raise serious professional communication concerns, and his weak numeric memory may impact operational tasks.\nCandidate\u202fD shows strong problem\u2011solving, concentration, and responsibility, but his hot temper, know\u2011it\u2011all attitude, and weak leadership raise doubts about his ability to foster a collaborative cockpit environment.\nTo solidify our decision, I propose we obtain any additional observations on Candidate\u202fC\u2019s in\u2011cockpit communication style and on Candidate\u202fA\u2019s crew\u2011cooperation behavior. These data points will help us confirm whether C\u2019s communication gaps are manageable and whether A can meet the teamwork expectations of the role.\nMETADATA_JSON:\n{\"agent\": \"agent_2\", \"vote\": \"Candidate C\"}"}
